laurel 2153: stage-1 SMMU tables for the Adreno 610, on by default (kill switch gpu_smmu=0); fallback = pass-through.
The GPU comes up in pass-through as in 2150 (the path topaz proved). Then, with the CP idle, context bank 0 switches to the
kernel's tables (one GPU address space for all clients; 32-bit GPU VA, 4 KiB pages, LPAE, Normal non-cacheable, HUPCF, no
fault interrupt) and checks itself: register read-back (SCTLR.M, TTBR0, CBAR0 type, S2CR0, SMR0), one CP fence through
the tables at VA = PA, one CP fence into an ordinary kernel page (above 4 GiB here) at a VA that is not its PA (a
reserved pool unit: a write that bypassed the tables lands there, never in MMIO, and fails the check), FSR 0.
Any failure: SCTLR back to pass-through, a fence checks the CP again, and 2150's backboardd-only rule stays.
Line: LAUREL GPU SMMU: on: stage 1, ... | off: <reason>: pass-through (GPU VA = PA), only backboardd may open the GPU.
GPU VA: 0x01000000-0x7fffffff allocator (scattered buffers, IOSurfaces); 0x80000000-0xffffffff VA = PA for contiguous
memory below 4 GiB (CP buffers, GPU pool buffers: the pool bookkeeping stays address = physical).
RedwoodGPU: pool first (write-combined, no cache upkeep), then ordinary pages (scattered, cached, the existing upkeep);
pool buffers and pool surfaces mapped write-combined in user space; RedwoodMaxBuffer = 256 MiB with the tables (64 MiB -
64 KiB in pass-through), LaurelGPUSMMU property; no TLBIALL on map (an SMMU caches no invalid entry; HakiOS does the
same), TLBIALL on unmap stays. SMMU faults: one line each (16 at most) in the 5 s statistics, FSR cleared.
SweetSurface: with the tables, every surface gets the 64 guard rows and no contiguous fallback is asked for.
Design and test steps: docs/laurel_sprout/gpu.md "SMMU stage 1 (2153)".

